/A 2808 0 R
/Alt (The lowest MAE values are observed for AG_Supply across all models, while NG_Users shows the highest MAE values, particularly for GM\(1,1\). This bar graph compares the Mean Absolute Error \(MAE\) of five prediction models&#x02014;GM\(1,1\), ARIMA, GM&#x02013;Markov, PSO-GM&#x02013;Markov, and WOA-GM&#x02013;Markov&#x02014;across six gas datasets, using a logarithmic vertical axis. For NG Supply, GM\(1,1\) is 50.7, ARIMA is 115.8, GM-Markov is 64.5, PSO-GM-Markov is 49.4, and WOA-GM-Markov is 49.4. For NG Users, MAE values are 17698, ARIMA is 784.2, GM-Markov is 422.4, PSO-GM-Markov is 366.8, and WOA-GM-Markov is 366.8. For AG Supply, GM\(1,1\) is 2.2, ARIMA is 10.0, GM-Markov is 2.2, PSO-GM-Markov is 2.2, and WOA-GM-Markov is 2.2. For AG Users, GM\(1,1\) is 40.3, ARIMA is 286.3, GM-Markov is 57.3, PSO-GM-Markov is 40.3, and WOA-GM-Markov is 40.3. For LPG Supply, GM\(1,1\) is 35.7, ARIMA is 158.0, GM-Markov is 38.8, PSO-GM-Markov is 34.7, and WOA-GM-Markov is 34.7. For LPG Users, GM\(1,1\) is 174.3, ARIMA is 663.1, GM-Markov is 221.0, PSO-GM-Markov is 174.7, and WOA-GM-Markov is 174.7. These results align with RMSE findings, confirming the WOA-optimized model delivers the most accurate and stable predictions for urban gas energy data.)
